    "texts": {
      "A": {
        "title": "How to Help a Choking Person",
        "text": "A person who is choking cannot breathe because something blocks the airway. They may hold their throat, be unable to speak or cough, and turn pale. If a person can still cough, encourage them to keep coughing. If they cannot, act quickly. Stand to the side and slightly behind them, lean them forward, and give up to five sharp blows between the shoulder blades with the heel of your hand. If this does not work, stand behind them, put your arms around their waist and give up to five abdominal thrusts: pull sharply inwards and upwards just above the belly button. The thrusts push air out of the lungs, like an artificial cough. Keep repeating the two steps and call your local emergency number. If the person becomes unconscious, start CPR. Different countries teach slightly different steps, and babies need a different method, so a first-aid course is the best way to learn."
      },
      "B": {
        "title": "Why We Choke",
        "text": "We use the same space at the back of the throat for breathing and for swallowing, and that is why we can choke. Below this space, the body has two tubes: the windpipe, which takes air to the lungs, and the food pipe, which takes food to the stomach. When you swallow, a small flap of tissue called the epiglottis folds down and covers the entrance to the windpipe, so the food goes the right way. Most of the time this works perfectly, in less than a second. But if you talk, laugh or hurry while you eat, a piece of food may reach the windpipe before the flap closes. The body reacts with a strong cough to push it out. If the piece is large enough to block the windpipe completely, the person cannot breathe and needs help at once. This is why it helps to chew well and to sit calmly when eating."
      }
    },
