    "texts": {
      "A": {
        "title": "Why CPR Keeps Blood Moving",
        "text": "When a person’s heart stops beating properly, blood stops moving around the body. The brain needs a constant supply of oxygen, and without it, brain cells begin to die within a few minutes. CPR, which stands for cardiopulmonary resuscitation, is a way to keep some blood moving until professional help arrives. The rescuer pushes hard and fast on the center of the chest, about five to six centimeters deep, at a rate of 100 to 120 pushes a minute. Each push helps to send blood, and the oxygen it carries, to the brain. CPR does not usually restart the heart. Its job is to buy time. That is why you should call your local emergency number first and start pushing at once. Many dispatchers can guide you by phone."
      },
      "B": {
        "title": "How an AED Works",
        "text": "An AED, or automated external defibrillator, is a portable machine that can give the heart an electric shock. You will find one in many airports, schools and malls, and anyone can use it. The machine speaks to you and shows what to do. First you turn it on and place two sticky pads on the person’s bare chest. The AED then checks the heart’s rhythm through the pads. If it finds a dangerous rhythm called ventricular fibrillation, in which the heart muscle only quivers instead of pumping, it tells everyone to stand clear and it delivers a shock. The shock can stop the chaos for a moment and give the heart a chance to restart a normal rhythm. If the heart has stopped completely, a shock does not help, so the AED will not give one. After any shock, you should start CPR again at once."
      }
    },
