      "A": {
        "title": "How Soap Kills Germs",
        "text": "Washing your hands with soap is better than using water alone, and the reason lies in the shape of soap molecules. Each soap molecule has two ends: one end likes water, and the other end likes fats and oils. Many germs, including some viruses and bacteria, are surrounded by a thin layer of fat. The oil-loving ends of soap molecules push into this layer and pull it apart, so the germ breaks. Soap also lifts dirt, oil and germs off the skin and holds them in tiny bubbles called micelles, and the running water washes them away. This takes time, so health experts say you should rub your hands for at least twenty seconds, including between your fingers and under your nails. Hand sanitizer with at least sixty percent alcohol also kills many germs, but it does not remove dirt."
      },
      "B": {
        "title": "How Germs Spread",
        "text": "Germs are tiny things, such as bacteria and viruses, that can cause illness. They cannot jump or fly by themselves, so they need a way to travel from one person to another. One way is through the air. When a sick person coughs, sneezes or even talks, they release tiny drops that contain germs, and a nearby person can breathe them in. Another way is by touch. A person with germs on their hands touches a doorknob, a phone or a table, and the next person touches the same surface and then their own face. The germs can enter the body through the nose, mouth or eyes. Germs can also travel in food, water or through the bite of an insect, such as a mosquito. Because of these routes, covering your cough, washing your hands and staying home when you are ill all help to stop the spread."
      }
