    "blurb": "why prices rise, why a little inflation is normal",
    "texts": {
      "A": {
        "title": "Why Prices Rise Over Time",
        "text": "Inflation means that prices in general go up over time, so the same money buys less than before. A fifty-peso snack that costs sixty pesos a few years later is a small example. There are two common causes. The first is demand: when many people want to buy things, but there is not enough to sell, sellers can ask for more. The second is cost: when the price of fuel, farm products or workers rises, businesses pass the extra cost on to customers. Bad weather, wars and supply problems can all push costs up. Economists measure inflation by following the prices of a typical basket of goods and services, such as rice, rent, transport and electricity, and then calculating the yearly change. Wages sometimes rise too, but not always as fast as prices."
      },
      "B": {
        "title": "Why a Little Inflation Is Normal",
        "text": "Most central banks do not try to keep prices perfectly still. They aim for a small, steady rise, often around two percent a year. Many countries use this level, and the Philippines sets a target range of two to four percent. There are good reasons for this. If prices fell, people might wait to buy things because they expect them to be cheaper next month. When many people do this, businesses sell less, and may cut jobs. Falling prices, called deflation, can also make debts harder to pay because the money you owe stays the same while your income shrinks. A little inflation gives the economy some space. It also allows a central bank to lower interest rates in a crisis, because rates have room to fall. But high inflation is harmful, since it makes prices hard to plan for and hurts people on fixed incomes."
      }
    },
