    "part": 61,
    "slug": "unseen-reading-part-61",
    "theme": "Banks",
    "blurb": "what a bank does with your deposit, why accounts are insured",
    "texts": {
      "A": {
        "title": "What a Bank Does With Your Deposit",
        "text": "When you put money in a bank, the bank does not keep all of it in a safe. Most banks keep only a small part as cash, and they lend much of the rest to other people and companies. A family may borrow to buy a house, or a business may borrow to buy equipment. The borrowers pay the bank interest, which is the price of using the money. The bank uses some of this income to pay you a small amount of interest on your savings, and it keeps the difference as profit. This system helps the economy because savings are turned into loans that people can use. Banks also must follow rules about how much cash they keep, called reserve requirements. This is possible because depositors rarely ask for all their money on the same day."
      },
      "B": {
        "title": "Why Bank Accounts Are Insured",
        "text": "Because a bank lends out most of its deposits, it cannot pay everyone back at once. In the past, when people feared that a bank was in trouble, they rushed to take out their money. This is called a bank run, and it can make even a healthy bank fail. In the 1930s, thousands of banks failed in the United States, and many families lost their savings. To prevent this, many countries created deposit insurance. In the Philippines, the Philippine Deposit Insurance Corporation, or PDIC, protects depositors. Since March 2025, it insures deposits up to one million pesos per depositor in each bank. If an insured bank closes, the PDIC repays depositors up to this limit. Because people trust that their money is safe, they have less reason to panic, so bank runs are now much rarer."
      }
    },
