    "part": 62,
    "slug": "unseen-reading-part-62",
    "theme": "Interest",
    "blurb": "how compound interest grows savings, how interest grows debt",
    "texts": {
      "A": {
        "title": "How Compound Interest Grows Savings",
        "text": "Interest is a payment for the use of money. Simple interest is paid only on the money you first put in. Compound interest is more powerful, because it is paid on the original money and on the interest that was added before. Imagine that you save 10,000 pesos at 10 percent a year. After one year you have 11,000 pesos. In the second year, the 10 percent is paid on 11,000, not on 10,000, so you earn 1,100 and have 12,100. The gains seem small at first, but they grow faster and faster as time passes. A simple guide called the Rule of 72 shows how long it takes to double your money: divide 72 by the interest rate, so at 6 percent it takes about 12 years. This is why starting to save early matters so much."
      },
      "B": {
        "title": "How Interest Makes Debt Grow",
        "text": "Compound interest works in the opposite direction when you owe money. If you borrow money and do not pay it back, the lender adds interest to what you owe, and next month interest is charged on the larger amount. Credit cards often have high rates, sometimes over twenty percent a year. Suppose you owe 10,000 pesos at 24 percent a year and pay nothing. By the Rule of 72, your debt would double in about three years. Paying only the minimum each month is also costly, because most of the payment goes to interest, and the balance falls very slowly. The best habit is to pay the full amount on time. If that is not possible, pay as much as you can above the minimum, and pay the loan with the highest rate first."
      }
    },
