    "blurb": "why false stories spread, how to check a source",
    "texts": {
      "A": {
        "title": "Why False Stories Spread Faster",
        "text": "In 2018, researchers at MIT studied about 126,000 stories shared on Twitter over more than ten years. They found that false stories reached more people and spread faster than true ones. A false story was about 70 percent more likely to be shared than a true one. Surprisingly, the main reason was not robots, but people. The researchers think that false stories are often more novel, or new and surprising, and people like to share news that nobody has heard before. Such stories also tend to cause strong feelings such as fear, anger or disgust, while true stories more often cause sadness or trust. Strong feelings make us want to react quickly, often before we check whether the story is real. The study looked only at stories that fact-checkers had examined, so it may not describe every kind of news."
      },
      "B": {
        "title": "How to Check a Source",
        "text": "When you find a surprising story online, a good habit is to ask three questions. Who is behind this information? What is the evidence? What do other sources say? Researchers at Stanford studied how professional fact-checkers work, and they found a clever method. Instead of staying on the page and studying it for a long time, fact-checkers quickly open new tabs and search for information about the website or the author. This method is called lateral reading. It works because a website can look professional, with a nice design and an official name, while the people behind it have a hidden purpose. Reading what others say about the source is faster and more reliable than judging it only by its appearance. Finally, check the date, because an old story can come back and look like new."
      }
    },
