    "part": 65,
    "slug": "unseen-reading-part-65",
    "theme": "Memory",
    "blurb": "why memories change, why eyewitnesses make mistakes",
    "texts": {
      "A": {
        "title": "Why Memories Change",
        "text": "Many people think of memory as a video that is stored in the brain and played back exactly as it happened. In fact, memory works more like a story that the brain rebuilds each time. When you remember an event, the brain puts together pieces from different places: images, sounds, feelings and facts. Some of the pieces may be missing, so the brain fills the gaps with guesses, with what you know about similar events, or with things you heard later. Research suggests that a memory can become flexible when you recall it, and then it is stored again, perhaps with small changes. Over many years, these small changes can add up. This is why two people can remember the same family party in different ways, and both can be completely sure. Feeling sure is not proof that a memory is correct."
      },
      "B": {
        "title": "Why Eyewitnesses Make Mistakes",
        "text": "Police and courts often depend on eyewitnesses, but memory research shows that witnesses can make serious mistakes. In a famous experiment in 1974, Elizabeth Loftus and John Palmer showed people films of car accidents and then asked how fast the cars were going. When the question used the word “smashed”, people guessed a higher speed than when it used the word “hit” or “contacted”. A week later, those who heard “smashed” were also more likely to say that they had seen broken glass, although there was none in the film. The wording of the question had changed their memory. Stress, a quick event and a weapon in the scene can also make details harder to remember. Today, careful police interviews avoid leading questions, and many courts warn jurors that a confident witness can still be wrong."
      }
    },
