    "texts": {
      "A": {
        "title": "Why Advertising Works on Us",
        "text": "Advertisers have many ways to make a product feel more attractive. One is repetition: when we see a name or a song again and again, it becomes familiar, and familiar things feel safe and good. Another is social proof. If an ad says that millions of people use a product, or shows happy customers, we tend to think that the product must be good, because we follow what others do. Advertisers also use famous people, whose success we would like to share, and short stories that connect a product to a feeling, such as friendship, freedom or comfort. Finally, many ads create a sense of urgency with words such as “today only” or “last chance”, which push us to decide quickly. Knowing these methods does not make us immune, but it can help us pause and ask what we really need."
      },
      "B": {
        "title": "How “Free” Apps Make Money",
        "text": "Many apps are free to download, but the companies that make them still need money to pay engineers, servers and staff. Most of them earn it in one of a few ways. The most common is advertising. The app shows you ads, and advertisers pay the company each time an ad is seen or clicked. To make ads more effective, an app may collect data about you, such as your age, your location and the topics you like, and use it to show ads that fit you. Other apps use a “freemium” model: the basic version is free, but extra features cost money, and a small share of users pay for them. Some games sell small items inside the game. So the real price of a free app is often your attention and your information, and it is wise to read the privacy settings before you agree."
      }
    },
