    "part": 67,
    "slug": "unseen-reading-part-67",
    "theme": "Biases",
    "blurb": "confirmation bias, anchoring",
    "texts": {
      "A": {
        "title": "Confirmation Bias: Seeing What We Expect",
        "text": "Confirmation bias is the habit of looking for, noticing and remembering information that agrees with what we already believe, while ignoring information that does not. Suppose a person believes that a certain football team is the best. That person will remember every win of the team and quickly explain away its losses as bad luck. In the 1960s, the psychologist Peter Wason gave people a number puzzle and found that most of them tested only examples that matched their first idea, instead of trying to prove it wrong. Today, confirmation bias is easy to see online: websites and social media often show us posts similar to those we liked before, so we may meet fewer different views. It does not mean that people are foolish. It is a mental shortcut. To reduce it, we can ask: what would change my mind, and what do people who disagree with me say?"
      },
      "B": {
        "title": "Anchoring: Why the First Number Sticks",
        "text": "Anchoring is a thinking trap in which the first number we see strongly influences our later judgment, even when the number is unrelated. In 1974, the psychologists Amos Tversky and Daniel Kahneman asked people to watch a wheel of fortune that was fixed to stop at 10 or at 65. Then they asked the people what percentage of the countries in the United Nations are in Africa. People who saw 10 guessed about 25 percent on average, but those who saw 65 guessed about 45 percent. Everyone had seen that the wheel was random, yet it still pulled their answers. Anchors are common in daily life: the “original price” crossed out on a sale tag, the first offer in a negotiation, or the price of the most expensive dish on a menu. To protect yourself, make your own estimate before you look at the number that others give."
      }
    },
