    "texts": {
      "A": {
        "title": "The Printing Press",
        "text": "Before the middle of the fifteenth century, books in Europe were copied by hand, usually by monks, and each one could take months. A book was so expensive that it was mostly owned by churches, universities and rich people. Around 1450, a German goldsmith named Johannes Gutenberg built a printing press that used movable metal letters. Printers could arrange the letters into a page, ink them, press them onto paper and then reuse the letters for the next page. Printing with movable type had been used earlier in Asia, but Gutenberg’s system was well suited to the alphabet, which needs only a few dozen letters. By about 1455, he had printed a famous Bible. Within fifty years, printing shops had opened in hundreds of towns, and millions of books were in circulation. Books became cheaper, more people learned to read, and new ideas traveled much faster."
      },
      "B": {
        "title": "From ARPANET to the Web",
        "text": "In the 1960s, the US Department of Defense funded a research network called ARPANET to connect computers at different universities. On 29 October 1969, a researcher at UCLA sent the first message to a computer at Stanford. The system crashed after the first two letters, “LO”, which were meant to start the word “LOGIN”. The network grew during the 1970s, and in 1983 it adopted common rules, called protocols, that let different networks join together into what we call the internet. The internet was still mostly used by scientists and universities. The change came in 1989, when Tim Berners-Lee, working at CERN in Switzerland, proposed the World Wide Web: a system of linked pages that anyone could read with a program called a browser. The Web became available to the public in the early 1990s, and within a few decades, billions of people were online."
      }
    },
