    "part": 69,
    "slug": "unseen-reading-part-69",
    "theme": "Time",
    "blurb": "why time zones exist, the International Date Line",
    "texts": {
      "A": {
        "title": "Why We Have Time Zones",
        "text": "Until the nineteenth century, every town set its clocks by the Sun. Noon was the moment when the Sun was highest, so noon in one town was a few minutes different from noon in the next town to the east or west. This was not a problem when travel was slow. But railways made it one. Each railway company used its own time, so a traveler might have to check several clocks, and timetables were confusing. On 18 November 1883, the railways of North America agreed on standard time and divided the continent into four zones, each one hour apart. The idea soon spread. In 1884, delegates from 25 countries met in Washington and chose Greenwich in England as the starting line for measuring longitude, which later helped the world to organize its zones. Today there are about 24 main time zones, though some countries use half-hour or forty-five-minute differences."
      },
      "B": {
        "title": "The International Date Line",
        "text": "When the Earth turns, it is daytime on one side and night on the other, so it cannot be the same date everywhere at the same moment. A line must be chosen where the date changes, and that line is the International Date Line. It runs from the North Pole to the South Pole, roughly along 180 degrees longitude, which is on the opposite side of the Earth from Greenwich. The line is not straight: it bends around islands and countries so that neighbors keep the same date. If you cross it from west to east, for example from Japan to Hawaii, you go back one calendar day. If you cross it from east to west, you go forward one day. In 2011, Samoa moved to the west side of the line and skipped Friday, 30 December, to be closer to its main trading partners, Australia and New Zealand."
      }
    },
