    "part": 70,
    "slug": "unseen-reading-part-70",
    "theme": "Writing and Numbers",
    "blurb": "how the alphabet spread, where our numbers came from",
    "texts": {
      "A": {
        "title": "How the Alphabet Spread",
        "text": "Most of the alphabets in use today can be traced back to systems that appeared more than 3,500 years ago. An early alphabet was used by peoples in the Middle East, and from about 1050 BC the Phoenicians, traders who sailed around the Mediterranean, used a set of 22 signs that stood for consonant sounds. The Greeks borrowed this system around the eighth century BC, and they made an important change: they used some signs for vowels. The word “alphabet” itself comes from the first two Greek letters, alpha and beta, and the Greek alpha began as a Phoenician sign for a consonant. The Etruscans in Italy took the Greek letters, and the Romans then adapted the Etruscan forms to write Latin. The Latin alphabet spread with the Roman Empire and later with European languages, which is why you can read these words today."
      },
      "B": {
        "title": "Where Our Numbers Came From",
        "text": "The digits 0 to 9 that we use today are often called Arabic numerals, but they began in India. Indian mathematicians developed a system in which the position of a digit shows its value, so that in 305, the 3 means three hundreds. They also treated zero as a number with its own rules. A famous book by the mathematician Brahmagupta, written in the year 628, explained how to calculate with zero. Scholars in the Islamic world, such as al-Khwarizmi in the ninth century, wrote about the system and spread it across the Arab world. In 1202, an Italian mathematician, Fibonacci, introduced it to Europe in a book called Liber Abaci. Many Europeans were slow to accept it, because Roman numerals were familiar, and the new numbers were thought to be easy to change. But merchants found them far easier for calculating, and by the sixteenth century they were in common use."
      }
    },
