    "theme": "First Money",
    "blurb": "the first coins, the first paper money",
    "texts": {
      "A": {
        "title": "The First Coins",
        "text": "Long before coins, people traded by barter, which means exchanging one thing for another, such as grain for cloth. Barter has a problem: you must find someone who has what you want and who also wants what you have. Over time, people used objects such as shells, salt and pieces of metal as a kind of money. The first known coins were made in Lydia, in what is now western Turkey, in about 600 BC. They were made of electrum, a natural mixture of gold and silver, and they were stamped with a symbol such as a lion to show that the ruler guaranteed their weight and value. This was a great improvement, because people no longer needed to weigh and test the metal every time. A little later, King Croesus of Lydia had coins made of pure gold and pure silver. The idea soon spread to the Greeks, the Persians and beyond."
      },
      "B": {
        "title": "The First Paper Money",
        "text": "Paper money began in China, where paper was invented around two thousand years ago. During the Tang dynasty, merchants sometimes left heavy coins with a trusted person and carried paper receipts. Then, in the early eleventh century, in the Song dynasty, traders in Sichuan began to issue paper notes called jiaozi because the iron coins used there were heavy and difficult to carry. Around 1024, the government took over the system and began to print official notes. At first, a note could be exchanged for coins at any time. But later, governments sometimes printed too many notes to pay for wars, and prices rose. When Marco Polo visited China in the thirteenth century, he described with wonder the paper money that the emperor used. Europe did not use paper money in any big way until the seventeenth century, when the first notes appeared in Sweden in 1661."
      }
    },
